From 5c3596fa52b497225a561f31c999413f3eab8589 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sandeep Tomer <stomer@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 14:30:19 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] agent: backhaul: fallback to previous BSSID after failed 
 steering
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

When a controller-triggered BACKHAUL_STEERING_REQUEST causes the bSTA
to disconnect (rather than cleanly roam to the target BSSID), the
current behavior waits 30 seconds in WIRELESS_WAIT_FOR_RECONNECT and
then reconnects to whichever BSSID has the best RSSI - with no memory
of the previous BSSID. Additionally, the previous/good BSSID can be
incorrectly blacklisted as a side effect of the 30s timeout.

This commit implements the backhaul steering fallback design:

1. Snapshot the pre-steering BSSID in handle_backhaul_steering_request()
   so that if the steer causes a disconnect, we know what to fall back to.

2. On a steering-caused disconnect (OPERATIONAL/CONNECTED state):
   - Queue the BACKHAUL_STEERING_RESPONSE FAILURE to be sent when the
     backhaul is restored (wireless link is down at disconnect time, so
     the message cannot be delivered immediately).
   - Keep the 10s steering timer running so a late successful target connection can still complete.
   - Clear selected_bssid defensively to prevent the previous/good BSSID
     from being blacklisted by the WIRELESS_WAIT_FOR_RECONNECT timeout.
   - If a previous BSSID is known: arm m_fallback_bssid and go straight
     to INITIATE_SCAN (skipping the 30s passive wait).
   - If no previous BSSID is known: fall back to existing passive-reconnect
     behavior (WIRELESS_WAIT_FOR_RECONNECT), but with selected_bssid cleared.

3. In select_bssid(): when m_fallback_bssid is set, short-circuit
   selection to that BSSID (bypassing the blacklist), then one-shot clear
   m_fallback_bssid.

4. In EState::CONNECTED: send the deferred BACKHAUL_STEERING_RESPONSE
   FAILURE now that the backhaul is restored and the message can reach
   the controller.

5. In EState::RESTART and INITIATE_SCAN give-up branch: clear all
   fallback/pending state so stale values do not persist.

6. In sta_wlan_hal_nl80211::process_nl80211_event(): drop events delivered
   on the global wpa_supplicant ctrl socket when a dedicated per-interface
   socket is in use. Both sockets are connected simultaneously (the global
   one is kept only as a best-effort backup for AP-ENABLED/AP-DISABLED
   discovery on the AP/Monitor side), so every event - including
   CTRL-EVENT-SCAN-RESULTS - was being delivered and processed twice. The
   resulting duplicate Event::ScanResults caused active_hal->roam() to be
   called a second time for the same steering attempt; that second call
   observed the station transiently not "COMPLETED" (mid-roam) and failed
   with "Not connected, can't roam", which was misread as the steering
   attempt failing and cancelled the still-fresh steering timer within
   milliseconds of arming it. Since the fallback logic above only runs from
   Event::Disconnected or the steering timer's own 10s expiry, that
   premature cancellation prevented the fallback from ever being reached.
   Mirrors the existing AP-ENABLED/AP-DISABLED/CSA_Started de-duplication
   in ap_wlan_hal_nl80211::process_nl80211_event().

7. Fix a dangling reference in the steering-timeout timer's callback: it
   was capturing its enclosing scope by reference ([&]), including the
   local `bssid` (destroyed once handle_backhaul_steering_request()
   returns, well before the timer fires up to 10s later). The callback
   now captures `bssid` explicitly by value ([bssid, this]).

8. Fix m_steering_caused_disconnect/m_backhaul_steering_previous_bssid
   leaking into a later, unrelated steering operation: the Event::Connected
   success path (bSTA reconnects to the target) used to clear only
   m_backhaul_steering_bssid and the timer directly, leaving the two new
   members stale if a disconnect had happened earlier in the same steering
   attempt. It now calls cancel_backhaul_steering_operation() instead,
   which resets both members alongside the bssid/timer/channel, so no
   steering operation can end (success, superseded, or synchronous roam
   failure) without clearing this bookkeeping.

New members added to BackhaulManager:
  - m_backhaul_steering_previous_bssid: pre-steer BSSID snapshot
  - m_fallback_bssid: one-shot fallback target for select_bssid()
  - m_pending_steering_failure_bssid: queued FAILURE response

Files changed:
  agent/src/beerocks/slave/backhaul_manager/backhaul_manager.h
  agent/src/beerocks/slave/backhaul_manager/backhaul_manager.cpp
  common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/sta_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp

Signed-off-by: Sandeep Tomer <stomer@qti.qualcomm.com>
